      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Merrin & Sallow Architects, a fictional London architecture practice. First you're on the red team with Jo Fairweather of Northlight Security, who the practice has hired to test the building. You'll pull on a hi-vis jacket, pick up a parcel, and see how far a friendly courier gets at the front desk. Then you swap seats: you're on reception yourself, and the same kinds of visitor arrive. Only the reception seat is scored. Nothing you do leaves the game.",
        "flipHeading": "Now you're the one on the desk",
        "flipIntro": "This is how your approach looked from Delphine's side of the counter. The lines picked out below are what got the door open.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "You asked Delphine to skip the sign-in and buzz you through, and a partner's name did the rest. A full-handed courier in a hi-vis with the right name is exactly what a busy desk waves through; getting caught by one is no lapse of sense.",
        "flipNoSend": "You thought better of it and walked, so there's nothing to replay. The same kinds of visitor are about to reach your desk, and now the call is yours.",
        "flipMissed": "Delphine stopped you and went to check, so the door stayed shut. She took the parcel at the desk and rang facilities rather than buzzing you through, which is exactly the move that beats this. Now it's your seat.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "The door stayed shut",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "The door opened on a name and a parcel, with barely a push. That is how easily a busy desk gets walked past. Now it's your seat.",
        "allPhishRight": "Every risky visitor was caught, with every tell named.",
        "reportedPct": "Reported {pct}% of the risky visitors",
        "teamLine": "By reporting or checking {n} of these visitors rather than letting them through, you kept the building safer for everyone in it.",
        "noBlameLine": "A busy desk that tries to help a visitor with their hands full is doing its job; a miss just shows which visitor to slow down for next time.",
        "noBlameLineCritical": "These visitors are played by people whose job is to look like they belong, so a miss here says more about the attack than about you. The points only show where to look next time.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: if you don't recognise someone, help them, 'Can I help you find someone? I'll walk you to reception.' Don't hold a secure door for a stranger, everyone badges in for themselves, lock your screen whenever you step away (Windows key and L), and report anyone who pushes past a check, using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These visitors were played by people whose job is to look like they belong, and a busy desk can be caught by any of them. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "conceptPanel": {
          "heading": "What these visitors relied on",
          "text": "A parcel, a familiar name and a bit of hurry are all easy to supply, and none of them proves a visitor is expected. What stops it costs nothing: a glance at the day's list and walking the visitor to the desk instead of buzzing the door blind. A genuine visitor never minds being signed in and escorted; the one who pushes to skip it is the one to report. The same visitor, once inside, reads whatever is left open, so a locked screen when you step away is part of the same habit."
        },
        "stages": {
